/-!
# Finiteness over K(t) for every transcendental t

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.finiteDimensional_adjoin_of_transcendental`
(`685f0eca-c73f-571c-b627-994c1364dfe4`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `bc4e1e56-5766-5df9-8200-673eaab4dc84`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $x \in F$ be an element such that $F$ is
finite-dimensional as a vector space over the intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin
K {x}`. Let $t \in F$ be transcendental over $K$, that is, $t$ is not algebraic over $K$
(`Transcendental K t`). The conclusion is that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field
$K(t) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {t}`. The hypothesis on $x$ is an instance assumption, and no
relation between $x$ and $t$ is required: $x$ is an arbitrary element witnessing finiteness over
some simple intermediate extension, and the assertion is that finiteness then holds over the
subfield generated by any transcendental element. (If $x$ happens to be algebraic over $K$ the
hypothesis forces $F$ to be algebraic over $K$, so no transcendental $t$ exists and the statement is
empty; the substantive case is $F$ a function field in one variable over $K$.)

This is the intermediate-field form of the standard fact that a function field of one variable is
finite over $K(t)$ for every $t$ transcendental over the constant field, independently of the chosen
generator. It is used throughout the curve-theoretic part of the development, for instance in the
treatment of reductions and of places, where a transcendental element is produced by other means and
finiteness over the field it generates is then needed.

